# The engine has to be on disk before the link, because it is what makes this fixture's subject reachable: a
# delivered engine compiles DoltLite's copy of the shim to nothing, leaving the sentinel as the only definition left
# to collide. Without delivery both shims compile in full and the link fails on the ordinary wrapper duplicates, so
# the step below would pass having tested the wrong collision.
> kyoNativesReport
$ exec sh -c 'set -eu; find target/kyo-natives -name "libkyo_doltlite.*" | grep -q . || { echo "no engine was delivered, so the link below would not reach the sentinel"; exit 1; }'
# The link must fail on the duplicate entry points, and leave no binary. A binary here would be one where every
# doltlite:// call reaches kyo-sql-sqlite's engine instead of DoltLite's.
-> nativeLink
$ exec sh -c 'set -eu; if find target -type f -perm -u+x -name consumer | grep -q .; then echo "a binary holding both engines was produced"; exit 1; fi'
# The positive control, without which the step above passes on any failure at all: a dependency that does not
# resolve or a Main that does not compile would read as the collision under test. With one engine dropped, the same
# application must link, so the only thing left that can fail the step above is the pairing.
> set libraryDependencies := libraryDependencies.value.filterNot(_.name.contains("doltlite"))
> nativeLink
$ exec sh -c 'set -eu; find target -type f -perm -u+x -name consumer | grep -q . || { echo "one engine alone must link"; exit 1; }'
